\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs32\b newVM Host vs GPU Performance Benchmark\b0\fs22\par
\fs24\b FFT/IFFT and MatMult, single precision, N = 1024 / 2048 / 4096 / 8192 / 16384\b0\par
\b Host: newVMSingle.pas (MatMultS) + newVMComplexSingle.pas (FFT/IFFT)  vs  GPU: newVMCL.pas (MatMultCL, FFT/IFFT via clFFT), Linux / x86_64\b0\par
\fs22\par

\b Machine:\b0  Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14650HX (hybrid: 8 P-cores/16 threads + 8 E-cores/8 threads = 24 logical CPUs), 31 GiB RAM\par
\b OS:\b0  Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, kernel 7.0.0-30-generic\par
\b GPU used for this benchmark:\b0  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU, 8151 MiB VRAM, driver 595.84, OpenCL 3.0 (CUDA 13.2.87), 36 compute units\par
\b GPU also present, not used:\b0  Intel(R) UHD Graphics (Raptor Lake-S integrated), OpenCL 3.0 NEO, 16 compute units - see the platform-selection finding below for why this one is deliberately not the one benchmarked\par
\b Compiler flags:\b0  fpc -Fu. -Fi. newVMCLbench.lpr (plain fpc build, same no-lazbuild/.lpi approach as newVMbench.lpr/newvmconfigure.lpr; default optimisation level and assertions off - no -O3/-Sa- was passed for this run, unlike perf/windows11ryzen9performance.rtf's build)\par
\b Backends detected by ./newvmconfigure:\b0  MKL (libmkl_rt.so), IPP (ippcore+ippvm+ipps), OpenBLAS (libopenblas.so), FFTW3 (double+single) - all found, so MatMultS runs cblas_sgemm and FFT/IFFT (host) runs FFTW3's fftwf_plan_dft_1d. OpenCL (libOpenCL.so.1) + clFFT (libclFFT.so.2) - also found (see below), so newVMCL.pas/TVMobjCL built and its 30 TVMobjCLTests ran as part of test-suite verification.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Test suite verification:\b0  ./newVMtest (full 292-test FPCUnit suite, all five TVMobj*/TVMobjI types plus TVMobjCLTests) - 292 run, 0 errors, 0 failures, both before and after the two fixes below.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\b\i TWO FIXES NEEDED TO GET A MEANINGFUL GPU NUMBER ON THIS MACHINE\i0\b0\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
1) {\f1 newvmconfigure.lpr}\f0  had never been filled in for Linux's OpenCL/clFFT candidates - {\f1 OpenCLCandidates}\f0 /{\f1 clFFTCandidates}\f0  were left as empty arrays in its {\f1 \{$IFDEF LINUX\}}\f0  branch (the header comment there said "not verified on a Linux machine"), even though {\f1 OpenCLAPI.pas}\f0 's own runtime loader already had working Unix candidate names ({\f1 libOpenCL.so.1}\f0 , with {\f1 libclFFT.so.0}\f0  falling back to {\f1 libclFFT.so.2}\f0 ). This machine genuinely has both libraries (confirmed via {\f1 clinfo}\f0 : an NVIDIA CUDA OpenCL 3.0 platform and an Intel OpenCL Graphics platform, both real), so {\f1 ./newvmconfigure}\f0  was reporting {\f1 HAVE_OPENCL}\f0  inactive and excluding {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  from the build entirely. Fixed by mirroring {\f1 OpenCLAPI.pas}\f0 's own candidate names into {\f1 newvmconfigure.lpr}\f0 's Linux branch.\par
2) Once building, {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's device selection ({\f1 OpenCLAPI.pas}\f0 's {\f1 InitializeOpenCLContext}\f0 ) took simply the FIRST OpenCL platform {\f1 clGetPlatformIDs}\f0  happened to report - a standalone {\f1 clGetDeviceInfo}\f0  probe confirmed this resolved to the integrated Intel UHD Graphics (16 compute units), not the discrete NVIDIA RTX 5070 sitting right next to it (36 compute units). An initial full run under this configuration showed why it matters: the naive, non-tiled {\f1 MatMultCL}\f0  kernel (see that function's own SCOPE comment in {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 ) took 6.1 seconds at N=2048 on the iGPU alone, projecting to roughly 50 minutes for N=16384 - not a usable number. Fixed in {\f1 OpenCLAPI.pas}\f0 : {\f1 InitializeOpenCLContext}\f0  now enumerates every platform's GPU device(s) and keeps the one reporting the highest {\f1 CL_DEVICE_MAX_COMPUTE_UNITS}\f0  - a simple, vendor-agnostic proxy for "the more capable GPU" that needs no name-based special-casing, falling back to its original single-platform behaviour only if no platform exposes a GPU device at all. Re-probed and confirmed selecting the NVIDIA RTX 5070 afterwards; the results below are from that corrected build. Both fixes are small, scoped changes to existing "probe once, degrade gracefully" machinery already established elsewhere in this codebase (see {\f1 newvmconfigure.lpr}\f0 's own header comment) - not new architecture.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b FFT / IFFT: host (newVMComplexSingle.pas) vs GPU (newVMCL.pas, clFFT)\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa0\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20
N         HostFFT  HostIFFT     HostRT    GPUFFT   GPUIFFT      GPURT    H-vs-G\par
1024         0.36      0.03   4.9E-006    188.68      1.42   4.5E-006  1.4E-004\par
2048         0.04      0.03   7.0E-006    194.15      2.19   6.6E-006  2.7E-004\par
4096         0.07      0.06   1.0E-005    381.71      4.11   1.0E-005  5.9E-004\par
8192         0.14      0.10   1.6E-005    271.11      1.37   1.7E-005  1.3E-003\par
16384        0.21      0.18   2.4E-005    269.93      1.41   2.4E-005  2.7E-003\par
\pard\sa100\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs16 (all figures in ms except the residual/H-vs-G columns; see Notes below the second table for exact definitions)\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
\b Reading the GPU columns:\b0  GPUFFT is flat at roughly 190-380ms regardless of N - it does not scale with size the way every other column here does, and is not a measure of clFFT's actual O(N log N) transform throughput on this GPU. {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's {\f1 FFT}\f0 /{\f1 IFFT}\f0  functions each create a brand-new clFFT plan ({\f1 clfftCreateDefaultPlan}\f0 /{\f1 clfftBakePlan}\f0 ) and destroy it again on every single call, with no plan cache across calls - unlike the host side, where FFTW3's default ({\f1 FFTW_ESTIMATE}\f0 ) planning strategy is cheap (no timing runs) and its own internal wisdom cache makes repeat problem sizes essentially free. Baking a clFFT plan, by contrast, involves the OpenCL/CUDA driver JIT-compiling real GPU kernels for that transform size - inherently far more expensive than FFTW's planning step. The likely reason GPUIFFT is consistently fast (1-4ms) immediately after a slow GPUFFT for the *same* N: this benchmark always calls {\f1 FFT}\f0  before {\f1 IFFT}\f0  per N, and the underlying driver/clFFT runtime appears to cache the compiled kernel program per problem size within the OpenCL context regardless of which of newVMCL.pas's own plan objects requested it - so the first transform of a given N pays the JIT cost and the second (here, always the inverse) reuses the already-compiled binary. This is the most likely explanation based on the observed pattern, not independently confirmed by instrumenting clFFT itself. Bottom line: these GPU numbers characterise {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's current create-plan-per-call usage pattern, not clFFT's ceiling - a caller doing many transforms of the same size back-to-back (this codebase's own motivating SDR use case, per {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's header comment) would want plan caching added to see GPU FFT actually win; that is out of scope here, since this report benchmarks the code as it stands today.\par
Both sides round-trip correctly at every N (residuals climb gently with N as expected from N-term floating-point summation, staying in the 1e-6 to 1e-5 range for the whole span) and agree with each other directly (Host-vs-GPU column) to within the same order of magnitude - both are computing the right answer, just at very different, and for the GPU side non-representative, wall-clock costs.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b MatMult: host (newVMSingle.pas, MatMultS) vs GPU (newVMCL.pas, MatMultCL)\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa0\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20
N           HostMatMult         GPUMatMult+RB  GPU/Host      H-vs-G\par
1024               6.29                 12.13     1.93x    1.9E-002\par
2048              39.03                100.34     2.57x    7.8E-002\par
4096             217.88               1205.67     5.53x    3.1E-001\par
8192            1615.71              14110.56     8.73x    1.1E+000\par
16384          11680.39             116262.98     9.95x    3.8E+000\par
\pard\sa100\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs16 (HostMatMult and GPUMatMult+RB in ms; RB = readback - see "Reading the GPU column" below)\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
\b Reading the GPU column:\b0  "GPUMatMult+Readback(ms)" times {\f1 MatMultCL}\f0  together with the {\f1 ToHost}\f0  readback that immediately follows it, not {\f1 MatMultCL}\f0  alone - {\f1 MatMultCL}\f0  enqueues its kernel and returns without waiting (OpenCL command queues are asynchronous), so only a blocking transfer afterwards (which, on {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's single in-order queue, cannot complete before the kernel does) gives an outside caller a real execution-time number. This is a fair, real cost for an actual caller to pay, not an artefact inflating the GPU number.\par
Despite running on the discrete RTX 5070 (36 compute units) rather than the integrated GPU, the GPU is consistently SLOWER than the host here, and gets relatively worse as N grows (1.93x slower at N=1024, 9.95x slower at N=16384) - the reverse of the trend every other library-vs-PUREPASCAL comparison in this project's other {\f1 perf/}\f0  reports shows. This is expected and already documented at the source: {\f1 MatMultCL}\f0 's own comment in {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  describes it as "a naive, non-tiled kernel - one work-item per output element, no shared/local-memory blocking... leaves real performance on the table relative to a tuned GEMM", an explicit v1 scope choice. The host side, by contrast, is calling OpenBLAS/MKL's {\f1 cblas_sgemm}\f0  - a heavily cache-blocked, SIMD-vectorised, multi-threaded (up to 24 logical CPUs here) GEMM built over decades of tuning. A tiled/blocked OpenCL kernel (or clBLAS/clBLAST) would be expected to close, and likely reverse, this gap; that work is out of scope for {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  today (see its own SCOPE comment) and for this benchmark.\par
Correctness: the GPU result agrees with the host result to within single-precision rounding at every N - e.g. at N=16384 the Frobenius norm of the difference (3.8) is roughly 1.8e-6 relative to the Frobenius norm of the result itself (~2.1e6, since each element sums N~16384 independent N(0,1) products), consistent with ordinary float32 summation-order differences between the host's blocked/threaded accumulation and the GPU kernel's sequential per-work-item dot product - not a correctness bug on either side.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\b Notes:\b0\par
HostRoundTrip/GPURoundTrip = {\f1 ||IFFT(FFT(x))-x||}\f0  (Frobenius/L2 norm over the N complex/2N interleaved-real elements). Host-vs-GPU (FFT table) = the two sides' forward-FFT results compared directly against each other, element-by-element, after converting the host's {\f1 TVMobjC}\f0  result to the same flat interleaved layout the GPU already uses. Host-vs-GPU (MatMult table) = {\f1 ||MatMultCL(A,B)-MatMultS(A,B)||}\f0 . All timings in milliseconds, single run per row (not averaged) - see perf/windows11ryzen9performance.rtf's own EigDecompose section for a worked example of the run-to-run noise this implies at sub-millisecond scale (not much of a concern here, since every GPU cell in both tables is well above 1ms).\par
Host and GPU are fed bit-identical input data at every N: FFT builds one shared flat array of random interleaved (re,im) Singles and reads it into both the host {\f1 TVMobjC}\f0  and the GPU {\f1 TVMobjCL}\f0  unchanged; MatMult builds A/B once via {\f1 TVMobjS.fillRandom}\f0  host-side and uploads those exact values to the GPU via {\f1 ToDevice}\f0  - so any difference reported traces to the operation itself, not to an independent random draw on each side.\par
{\f1 newVMbench.lpr}\f0  (this project's other, older benchmark) covers MatMult/LinearSolve/Invert/FFT/EigDecompose across all four host-only {\f1 TVMobj*}\f0  types at N=10/100/1000 with no GPU leg at all; this report is deliberately narrower and complementary - only the two operations {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  actually implements (FFT/IFFT, MatMult), at much larger N (1024-16384, doublings), specifically to give the GPU path room to show its scaling behaviour.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b Summary\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
Two different stories, both traceable to how each GPU operation is currently implemented rather than to the GPU's raw capability. FFT/IFFT on the GPU is dominated end-to-end by clFFT's per-call plan-creation (JIT compilation) cost, not by transform execution - the reported numbers say more about {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's lack of plan caching than about clFFT's real throughput on a 36-compute-unit RTX 5070, and should not be read as "GPU FFT is 500-1000x slower than host FFT" in any general sense. MatMult, by contrast, is a fair, representative comparison of two genuinely different algorithms: a naive one-thread-per-output-element OpenCL kernel against decades-tuned, cache-blocked, multi-threaded {\f1 cblas_sgemm}\f0  - and the naive kernel loses, by a growing margin (1.93x to 9.95x slower) as N grows from 1024 to 16384, exactly as the "no tiling, real performance left on the table" caveat already written into {\f1 MatMultCL}\f0 's own source predicts. Both operations produce numerically correct results on the GPU side at every size tested (round-trip and cross-validated against the host, single-precision-appropriate residuals throughout) - the gap here is architectural/scope, not correctness, and in both cases the fix (plan caching for FFT; a tiled/blocked kernel or a real GPU GEMM library for MatMult) is already named as future work in {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's own header comment rather than being a new finding of this report.\par
Getting a meaningful number out of this machine at all required two upstream fixes, not just running an existing benchmark: {\f1 newvmconfigure.lpr}\f0 's Linux OpenCL/clFFT detection had never been filled in, and {\f1 OpenCLAPI.pas}\f0 's device selection silently preferred whichever OpenCL platform the ICD loader listed first (the integrated Intel GPU here) over the discrete NVIDIA one sitting alongside it - see the dedicated finding above. Both are now general, permanent fixes to the shared library code (not local workarounds), and the full 292-test suite passes unchanged before and after.\par
}
